Subject: Tweak: Thunder Island walls
Upstream-base: 9046e67aa2077a7eb4ed6e28c9df3c1d3b368f84

Thunder Island has the electric fence of the other stadiums only around its
goals; along its sides the island ends. The field's walls are the same
planes as in every stadium (CreateStaticPhysicsPrimitives), but on Thunder
Island alone the game lets things through them along the sidelines:

- The ball passes the sides above their low edge (0.36) and falls off past
  them (PhysicsAIBall::Contact, QueueBallFall); shells, bananas, Bob-ombs and
  the Chain Chomp do likewise (PhysicsShell, PhysicsBanana, PhysicsNPC).
- A fielder without the ball, or deking, passes the sides
  (PhysicsCharacter::Contact) and falls off once past a sideline
  (cFielder::CollideWithWallCallback, fn_80046244), to come back after a
  while. Launched players lose their wall collisions and land or fall
  outside. The wind and teleport dekes may take players past the sides, and
  the Chain Chomp may run past them; every other stadium keeps them inside
  (cField::FixOutOfBoundsPosition).
- Nobody is electrocuted along the sides or while launched
  (cFielder::CanGetElectrocuted), and the ball and items hitting the sides
  make no fence sparks or sound.
- The CPU keeps further from the sidelines (cGame's boundary avoidables), and
  a controlled player's steering avoids them, as with the High Voltage cheat.

With "Thunder Island walls" enabled, all of these take the other stadiums'
path in an offline match on Thunder Island (ThunderIslandWalls in
GameInfo.cpp): the sides are an electric fence like the goal ends, invisible
but sparking where the ball and items hit it, and players knocked into it are
electrocuted (issue #54). The game's Power and High Voltage cheats then apply
to the sides as well, and the Waluigi Striker Challenge, played there,
follows the tweak like any offline match. Online and LAN matches keep the
open sides, as does the game with the tweak off.

Flying debris passes a player while the fence electrocutes him
(cFielder::CollideWithWindDebrisCallback). Debris that throws a player into
a side still overlaps him there; it threw him again at once, into the fence
again, several times a frame, until the game ran out of sound handles and
crashed in PlaySound. The original island never gets there, as a launched
player has no walls and cannot be electrocuted. Otherwise the island's look,
its wind, lightning and flying debris, and the shadows it hides near its
edges are unchanged.

